      "writtenOn": "2024-11-01T19:15:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This particular case, I don\u0027t know, but aren\u0027t there many instances where we never unregister the `Dumpable`? But even if you do unregister, it\u0027s easy to fall into the broken case today, especially as Dumpable is an interface, e.g.,\n\n```\nprivate final TypeThatImplementsDumpable foo \u003d new TypeThatImplementsDumpable() {\n    @Override void fooMethod() {}\n};\n...\nvoid start() {\n  registerDumpable(foo);  \u003c-- This breaks currently\n}\nvoid stop()\n  unregisterDumpable(foo.getName());\n}\n```",
      "parentUuid": "57664e1a_7932040f",
